It all makes good sense to me, except...

> * Wishing pills are (supposed to be) very rare. Unlike the others,
> they'll never pour forth from horns of plenty.

...This.

I know NHINRL, but I don't see any logical mechanism at all by which
one could possibly get a wish from a pill. Even if there IS some teeny
tiny genie sitting there inside that pill, it's not going to be
terribly happy when the pill dissolves in your stomach and it is
suddenly submerged in acid.

Oh, wait. Is this perhaps a reference to one of the books in the Wizard
of Oz series? The one where the guy eats the wishing pill, gets a
horrible stomachache, then wishes he'd never eaten the thing (thereby
causing the pill to reappear?) Yes? No? Even if it is, it didn't make a
lot of sense to me then, and it still doesn't now. (If it IS a
reference to that book, on the other hand, eating a wishing pill should
give you a stomachache and induce some damage.)

One more thing: placebo pills should give you a small nutritional
value, since they're often made of sugar.

